WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
Musk's Raptor v3 simplifications and xai's new power plant plans both target the same bottleneck, scaling reliable hardware and power for orbital compute. This is spacex daily episode 93. Elon Musk shared on a subreddit that the latest version of the Raptor engine has had several parts taken out compared to the previous model. The goal remains the same thrust level even after these removals. People in the discussion think this will help make engines

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:45.260
faster to build and cut down on things that could go wrong during tests. The post did not name which parts were removed or by how much the count dropped. It showed up in the the spacex lounge subreddit area where users compared the designs.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:01:05.020
The xai team is working with Duson on a power plant meant to provide electricity just for their big ai training setups. Reports indicate this is still in the early stages with no numbers on size or when it will start. The idea is to secure steady power for their large computing facilities over time.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:19.400
North details on where it would be built or when construction begins have come out yet. The engineering reality is that cutting down on engine components brings direct benefits to weight, reliability and how quickly units can be produced for the vehicle.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:39.420
Removing brackets, seals and extra flow paths, trims potential leak sites and inspection steps during assembly. Each saved kilogram on one engine scales across the full set of engines on a starship vehicle which improves the margin available for reaching orbit or lowers the propellant needed for a given velocity change.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:56.100
Production moves faster because fewer castings and machined joints require checks before the engine reaches the test stand or the integration bay. Qualification testing will show whether the simpler layout holds up without introducing new issues at the combustion chamber or turbo pump.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:15.760
A similar pruning of manifolds happened when the Merlin engine moved to its vacuum variant, raising reliability while shortening the build cycle and keeping specific impulse steady. The idiot index tracks how many times the finished turbo pump costs over the raw alloys, titanium and machining hours that go into it.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:39.460
Each removed bracket or seal narrows that gap by cutting both material scrap and labor hours. The same cost control idea shows up in the planned power plant where dedicated generation avoids buying electricity at peak rates and reduces price swings for training runs. Up front capital buys steadier utilization across the computing clusters without risk of power shortfalls.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:49.800
When the same logic moves to orbital data centers every kilogram and watts saved on the ground matters even more because cooling in space carries its own mass penalties.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:58.180
The Raptor changes therefore test the hardware discipline needed to support space-based computing on the timeline discussed for later this decade.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:07.160
The Federal Aviation Administration is weighing rule changes that would let SpaceX and other operators skip some environmental reviews for launch sites.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:21.580
Current permitting steps add months to the schedule for building or upgrading pads. The proposal would cover both new construction and existing facilities that handle vertical launches. No timeline for a final decision appears in the coverage yet.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:42.740
4 IG Group and SpaceX have started offering Starlink mobile service in parts of Europe. The offering focuses on maritime and land mobile customers who need portable terminals. Early availability covers select countries and draws on the existing satellite network. No details on pricing or exact performance numbers have been released.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:55.540
Stoke space and relativity space are moving forward with construction at launch complexes 14 and 16 on Cape Canaveral. The two sites sit next to current SpaceX pads at the Space Force Station.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:03.740
Work centers on foundations and support buildings ahead of planned first flights next year. No vehicle integration dates have been released.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.300
SpaceX plans to shift remaining Starlink missions from Florida over to Starship instead of Falcon 9.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:19.360
The move matches Starship's larger payload capacity for constellation work. Internal documents reviewed by observers show the change under consideration.

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:33.560
It would open Falcon 9 vehicles for other payloads. NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope lifted off on a Falcon Heavy. The launch used direct injection to place the telescope in a Sun Earth L2 Halo orbit.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:56.560
The mission supports wide field infrared surveys from that stable location. The flight took place on schedule according to the Week in Review Summary. A Falcon 9 sent 27 Starlink satellites into orbit from Cape Canaveral and landed on a drone ship. The flight followed the standard Block V profile. Video of the Ascent and Recovery appeared in coverage of the mission.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:06.740
The satellites joined the rest of the constellation in low Earth orbit. Before we go, watch for how the new power plant plans line up with permitting changes at the Cape.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.520
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

